window.LEARN_CONTENT=window.LEARN_CONTENT||{};window.LEARN_CONTENT["a-54e559ed4245e801"]={"zh":"<p><a id=\"chatgpt-work-local-security\"></a></p> <h1 id=\"chatgpt-work\">ChatGPT Work 本地安全</h1> <blockquote> <p>如需完整文档索引，请参阅 <a href=\"https://learn.chatgpt.com/llms.txt\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">llms.txt（需联网）</a>。在页面 URL 末尾追加 <code>.md</code>，即可获取该页面的 Markdown 版本。</p> </blockquote> <p>ChatGPT Work 可以使用用户电脑上经批准的文件、应用程序和浏览器会话来完成本地任务。访问能力取决于工作区权限、用户现有的账户访问权限、操作系统权限、应用程序审批以及受支持的设备策略。</p> <p>如需了解安全控制概况，请参阅 <a href=\"https://cdn.openai.com/pdf/chatgpt-work-enterprise-security.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">ChatGPT Work 企业安全白皮书（需联网）</a>。</p> <p>本地功能是否可用，取决于受支持的桌面应用、操作系统、工作区权益、角色权限、设备策略和产品推出情况。</p> <p><a id=\"security-at-a-glance\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_1\">安全要点</h2> <ul> <li> <p>本地步骤通过用户电脑上的 ChatGPT 桌面应用运行。启用 Work Cloud 本地电脑访问后，OpenAI 云端负责协调任务；需要本地执行的步骤会由已连接的电脑完成。</p> </li> <li> <p>本地 Work 和托管式 Work 可用的控制项，取决于工作区配置和功能推出情况。</p> </li> <li> <p>文件访问、Computer Use、浏览器和已连接应用使用不同的权限与审批机制。</p> </li> <li> <p>如果浏览器或应用程序已经登录公司系统，它可能会使用该现有账户所拥有的权限。</p> </li> <li> <p>受支持的受管设备策略可以限制本地功能，但不会取代工作区访问控制。</p> </li> <li> <p>对于 Business、Enterprise 和 Edu 工作区，由受涵盖的 OpenAI 服务处理的工作区数据会在传输中和静态存储时加密；默认情况下，这些数据不会用于训练 OpenAI 模型。</p> </li> <li> <p>本地文件、任务上下文、浏览器数据、已连接系统中的记录和审计事件，可能遵循不同的存储与保留规则。</p> </li> </ul> <p><a id=\"where-local-tasks-run\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_2\">本地任务在哪里运行</h2> <p>本地执行通过用户电脑上的桌面应用访问经批准的资源。启用 Work Cloud 本地电脑访问后，OpenAI 云端负责协调任务；某一步需要本地执行时，云端会调用已连接的电脑。执行这些步骤时，电脑必须保持联网并处于连接状态。</p> <p>Work Cloud 本地电脑访问仅适用于启用同步后创建的任务。现有任务（包括项目中的任务）会保留原有模式：仅在本地运行，或仅在云端运行且无法访问本地文件。要使用此功能，请新建任务。</p> <p>如果新一轮对话开始时电脑不可用，符合条件且已启用 Work Cloud 本地电脑访问的现有任务可以继续在云端容器中运行。云端容器无法访问不可用电脑上的文件或工具，也不会强制执行适用于本地执行的企业要求。任务不能在某一轮执行途中从本地切换到云端。</p> <p>本地文件可以继续保存在设备上，但为了完成任务，相关文件片段、提示词、屏幕截图、浏览器内容或工具结果仍可能发送到 OpenAI 服务。本地执行不代表离线运行，也不代表模型推理只发生在设备上。</p> <p><a id=\"files-and-device-access\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_3\">文件和设备访问</h2> <p>本地任务可以处理用户提供或允许访问的信息，包括受支持的文件、应用内容、浏览器会话和已授权的连接系统。具体访问能力取决于用户已有的权限，以及管理相应功能的控制项。</p> <p>授予 Work 本地访问权限，并不会自动批准所有应用程序、授予管理员权限，或绕过用于访问其他系统的账户权限。经批准的共享连接，其权限可能与用户个人账户不同。</p> <p>直接读取文件、编辑文件和运行 Shell 命令，遵循任务的沙箱与审批设置。Computer Use 会在经批准的原生应用程序中、按照该应用的权限访问内容。限制直接文件访问，并不能单独证明应用程序可以打开的文件也受到了相同限制。</p> <p><a id=\"computer-use-and-application-approvals\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"computer-use\">Computer Use 和应用审批</h2> <p>工作区权限决定哪些人可以使用 Work Local。管理员策略还可以进一步限制 <a href=\"#/article/a-7be3f970bd43699c\">Computer Use</a> 能操作哪些原生应用程序，以及应用审批能否保存供后续会话使用。所需的操作系统权限和应用审批仍然适用。任务使用某个应用或已登录账户时，可访问的信息和可执行的操作由该账户的权限决定。</p> <p>管理员策略允许某个应用程序，并不代表所需插件已安装、操作系统权限已授予，或仍需审核的操作已经获准。支持的控制项和配置示例，请参阅<a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e?anchor=control-browser-and-computer-use\">受管浏览器与 Computer Use 控制</a>。</p> <p>在 macOS 上，屏幕录制权限允许 Computer Use 查看应用内容；辅助功能权限允许它点击、输入和导航。受支持的 macOS 任务可以在后台运行。在 Windows 上，Computer Use 操作的是当前处于活动状态且可见的桌面；当用户继续使用同一个会话时，它不能在后台运行。</p> <p>用户可以随时停止任务。Computer Use 无法批准操作系统安全提示、以管理员身份进行身份验证，也不能自动操作终端应用程序或 ChatGPT 本身。</p> <p><a id=\"locked-devices\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"_4\">已锁定的设备</h3> <p>受支持的 macOS 配置可以选择允许经批准的 Computer Use 任务在 Mac 锁定后继续运行。是否可用取决于应用版本、功能推出情况、适用要求以及是否符合远程控制资格。</p> <p>启用 Locked Use 会安装一个 macOS 授权插件；在一次活动且受信任的 Computer Use 操作期间，该插件可以临时解锁 Mac。临时解锁时，ChatGPT 会覆盖所有显示器。如果检测到本地键盘或指针输入，它会重新锁定 Mac，并暂停自动解锁，直到用户手动解锁。请参阅 <a href=\"#/article/a-7be3f970bd43699c?anchor=locked-use\">Locked Use 的工作方式</a>。</p> <p>管理员可以禁止用户在受管 Mac 上启用 Locked Use。此要求不会关闭已经启用的 Locked Use。Windows 上的 Computer Use 需要桌面处于活动且未锁定状态。请参阅 <a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e?anchor=restrict-locked-computer-use\">Locked Use 限制</a>。</p> <p><a id=\"browser-sessions-and-existing-sign-ins\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_5\">浏览器会话和现有登录状态</h2> <p>Work Local 不会自动获得所有浏览器或公司账户的访问权限。访问能力取决于所用浏览器、已登录账户以及相应浏览器体验要求的审批。</p> <table> <thead> <tr> <th>浏览器路径</th> <th>会话与安全边界</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td><a href=\"#/article/a-a7de5b39bd26a809\">桌面应用内浏览器</a></td> <td>使用与用户常规浏览器分开的配置文件。用户可以在该配置文件中登录。在受支持的客户端上，受管 Browser Use 策略会限制代理可访问的网站和可用的浏览器功能。内置浏览器无法自动上传文件。</td> </tr> <tr> <td><a href=\"#/article/a-a7459570776840aa\">Chrome 扩展程序</a></td> <td>在扩展程序和网站访问都获准时，可以与现有标签页及已登录账户交互。在受支持的客户端上，同一套受管 Browser Use 策略也适用于通过扩展程序执行的代理操作。</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Computer Use 操作浏览器</td> <td>将浏览器作为经批准的原生应用程序来操作，并可使用已登录的账户。<strong>Browser Use 的网站和功能策略不适用于此路径。</strong>此路径仍受原生应用策略、操作系统权限、应用审批和已登录账户权限约束。</td> </tr> </tbody> </table> <p>受支持的 Browser Use 策略可以限制网站访问、上传、下载、浏览器历史记录访问，以及用于浏览器调试的完整 Chrome DevTools Protocol (CDP) 访问。管理员还可以限制自动审批审核、已保存的审批，以及网站访问审批的有效时长。用户批准不能推翻管理员的拒绝。请参阅<a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e?anchor=control-browser-and-computer-use\">受管浏览器控制</a>。</p> <p>托管云浏览器与用户本地浏览器分开，不会自动继承本地浏览器已有的登录状态。Enterprise 或 Edu 工作区无法通过云浏览器登录网站。</p> <p><a id=\"apps-plugins-and-connected-accounts\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_6\">应用、插件和已连接账户</h2> <p>已连接的应用可以访问另一个系统中的信息或执行其中的操作。插件可以把某个应用用作底层工具。让插件可用，并不代表所需应用已启用、账户已授权，或所有操作都获准。</p> <p>插件和应用的默认状态取决于方案，以及工作区是新建还是已存在。新的 Enterprise 和 Edu 工作区会默认启用一组选定的应用；这些默认设置不会更改现有工作区设置，也不适用于 Healthcare 工作区。一般而言，Enterprise 和 Edu 工作区中的新插件和新应用默认处于禁用状态；Business 应用默认启用。管理员可以更改其可用性。请参阅<a href=\"https://help.openai.com/en/articles/11509118\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">当前插件和应用默认设置（需联网）</a>以及 <a href=\"#/article/a-18ad336d66dd572e\">ChatGPT Work 概览</a>。</p> <p>任务使用已连接系统之前，请确认工作区允许使用该应用及任何所需插件、连接已获授权，并且连接账户有权访问所请求的信息或执行相应操作。不同集成的只读设置、允许的操作和确认要求可能不同。</p> <p>提供浏览器或 Computer Use 能力的插件，需要单独确认其可用性并完成安装审核。让插件可用，并不会覆盖受管浏览器或原生应用策略、操作系统权限或必要审批。仅限桌面应用的插件和本地提供的工具可能采用不同的安装流程。请参阅<a href=\"#/article/a-20bc98306a44b420\">插件控制</a>。</p> <p><a id=\"personal-and-shared-connections\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"_7\">个人连接和共享连接</h3> <p>个人连接使用连接用户在源系统中的权限。共享连接或代理自有连接使用连接账户的权限，其范围可能大于用户本人的访问权限。</p> <p>共享账户应仅开放必要的数据和操作，限制可使用该账户的人员，并应用受支持的操作控制或确认机制。连接系统中的记录仍受该系统自身的权限和保留政策约束。</p> <p><a id=\"administrator-access-and-managed-device-policies\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_8\">管理员访问权限和受管设备策略</h2> <p>使用 Work Cloud 本地电脑访问时，请同时检查工作区的同步权限，以及 Agent Security 中对本地执行的要求。</p> <p>对于本地执行，MDM 和旧版受管设备要求的优先级高于 Agent Security；设备系统要求文件的优先级低于 Agent Security。在同一项策略中，操作系统专属环境覆盖的优先级高于适用于所有操作系统的环境覆盖，后者又高于 Global。不同策略之间，优先级更高的策略会获胜，即使较低优先级策略更具体也一样。部分要求具有字段专属的合并规则。</p> <p>使用 Policy API 管理 Global 设置；管理 Local 或 Codex Cloud 设置时，请使用 Agent Security 界面。迁移后现有 Global API 工作流仍可使用。请测试脚本和 Terraform 集成，并确认策略分配和优先级顺序没有改变。</p> <p>对于启用了本地访问的 Work 和 dots，启用托管策略后，受支持的 Global 策略会通过共享云端协调器生效。适用的本地 <code>requirements.toml</code> 要求约束已连接电脑上的执行。Work 云端容器和 dots 云端电脑使用各自的执行配置和要求，而非其他执行器类型使用的托管环境配置包。本地执行限制不会自动应用到这些云端电脑。请分别检查云端功能权限，并单独测试本地和云端执行。</p> <p>启用同步前，请检查以下要求：</p> <ul> <li> <p><strong>仅通过 MDM 下发的策略。MDM 会将策略下发到设备。启用同步前，请先为受支持的本地执行要求创建 Agent Security 基线。</strong></p> </li> <li> <p><strong>企业钩子。</strong> 在工作区启用时，Work Cloud 本地电脑访问支持由管理员定义、在云端协调器（orchestrator）上运行的 MCP 钩子，用于受支持的生命周期和工具事件。云端协调不支持命令钩子，也不支持来自本地配置或插件的钩子，即使工具在本地执行也一样。当协调和执行都在本地进行时，现有的受支持钩子仍可用于仅限本地的 Work 和 Codex 线程。管理员仍可在 Agent Security 中为这些工作流配置受支持的托管钩子。</p> </li> <li> <p><strong>审计。</strong> 依赖这些钩子之前，请测试回调连接、确认能够收到的事件，并检查失败会如何影响任务。MCP 钩子不能提供完整的 Compliance API 审计记录。</p> </li> </ul> <p>请在 <strong>工作区设置</strong> &gt; <strong>权限与角色</strong> 中查看 Work 控制项。本地 Work 与托管 Work 是否显示为单独的权限，取决于工作区配置和功能推出情况。更多说明请参阅 <a href=\"#/article/a-b498643ba7f3888a\">Work 管理员常见问题</a>。</p> <p>仅为每个用户或用户组启用已批准的执行环境；更改后请验证实际生效的访问权限。</p> <p>工作区权限决定谁可以使用 Work。管理员还可以通过 <code>requirements.toml</code> 中定义的强制要求，限制受支持的桌面功能。根据部署方式，这些要求可以通过工作区托管配置、系统级配置文件或受支持的 macOS 移动设备管理工具下发。</p> <p>用户不能覆盖强制要求。受管默认设置则用于设定初始值，用户可能仍可以更改。强制要求和受管默认设置都不能取代工作区角色权限或操作系统权限。</p> <p>使用现有本地配置路径的客户端，会在加载配置时载入受管要求。后台刷新可以为之后加载配置准备云端要求；macOS MDM 设置变更则会在客户端下次启动时读取。本地策略变更后，请重启相应的本地客户端并核实实际设置。</p> <p>关闭 Work Cloud 本地电脑访问会中断当前正在运行的轮次。用户可以在现有云端对话中开始新一轮；新一轮会自动使用 Work Cloud，但无法访问本地文件。</p> <p>上述本地配置加载方式并不能说明启用 Work Cloud 本地电脑访问的运行中任务何时会应用云策略变更。依赖策略更新前，请确认任务实际生效的控制项。</p> <table> <thead> <tr> <th><strong>受管设置</strong></th> <th><strong>安全用途</strong></th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td><code>features.computer_use = false</code></td> <td>禁用原生 Computer Use 功能。</td> </tr> <tr> <td><code>allow_appshots = false</code></td> <td>禁止受支持的 Appshot 捕获。</td> </tr> <tr> <td><code>features.in_app_browser = false</code></td> <td>禁用桌面应用内置的浏览器。</td> </tr> <tr> <td><code>features.browser_use = false</code></td> <td>禁用受支持的 Browser Use 功能。外部 Browser Use 使用单独的标志。</td> </tr> <tr> <td><code>features.browser_use_external = false</code></td> <td>禁用代理通过受支持的浏览器扩展程序执行 Browser Use。</td> </tr> <tr> <td><code>features.apps = false</code> 或 <code>features.plugins = false</code></td> <td>限制受支持的已连接应用或插件。</td> </tr> <tr> <td><code>computer_use.allow_locked_computer_use = false</code></td> <td>禁止用户在受管 Mac 上启用 Locked Use；这不会关闭已经启用的 Locked Use。</td> </tr> </tbody> </table> <p>以上是限制示例，并非启用状态的列表。省略某项功能要求后，客户端、平台、功能推出情况和用户设置仍会按通常规则生效。Browser Use 功能标志、内置浏览器窗格和原生 Computer Use 是不同的控制项；请分别检查每一种浏览器路径。</p> <p>可用设置和下发方式取决于客户端、操作系统、工作区和部署配置。请在具有代表性的受管设备上检查限制。受支持的策略设置、配置示例和 MDM 设置说明，请参阅<a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e\">受管配置</a>。</p> <p><a id=\"local-networking-and-private-resources\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_9\">本地网络和私有资源</h2> <p>Work Local 可以通过浏览器、原生应用、已连接应用或其他受支持的工具访问公司信息。不同路径适用的网络控制各不相同；能够连接公司 VPN，本身并不代表任务获准使用内部资源。</p> <p>受管网络要求和 Browser Use 来源站点策略是两项独立检查。在依赖网络限制来约束 Browser Use 之前，请在部署使用的应用版本和操作系统上分别检查这两项设置。浏览器策略检查不代表浏览器流量经由命令网络代理路由；Browser Use 策略也不管理原生应用的网络流量。</p> <p><code>[experimental_network]</code> 下的受管网络要求仍属实验性功能，可能发生变化。Windows 支持范围有限；将其应用于 Windows 用户前，请先测试确切的客户端和环境。仅配置域名规则不会启用受管代理；必须设置 <code>experimental_network.enabled = true</code>，而且这也不会覆盖禁用网络的沙箱设置。</p> <p>请检查工作流程所需的连接、登录账户、目标位置和操作。配置详情与平台限制请参阅<a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e?anchor=configure-network-access-requirements\">网络访问要求</a>。</p> <p><a id=\"data-handling-and-retention\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_10\">数据处理和保留</h2> <p>对于 Work Cloud 本地电脑访问，即使某一步在电脑上执行，整个工作流仍包含云端协调。请将对话数据和任务上下文，与工具使用的本地文件分开评估。不能据此推断本地执行意味着数据只存储在设备上，也不能推断文件夹中的每个文件都会被上传。</p> <p>Work Cloud 本地电脑访问不提供严格的零数据保留（ZDR）。数据驻留和推理驻留仅覆盖符合条件的内容，以及受支持的工作负载、地区和配置。Enterprise Key Management（EKM）仅覆盖符合条件工作区中受支持的存储内容。Work 不支持阿联酋推理驻留。推广前请确认工作区的存储、保留和审计覆盖范围。如果组织要求 ZDR，请勿启用此功能。如果任何云策略启用了 <code>enforce_residency</code>，Work 和 dots 的 <strong>允许本地电脑访问（Allow local computer access）</strong> 都会被禁用。此安全措施不会配置工作区数据驻留，也不会单独关闭 Work Cloud 或 dots。</p> <p>应针对具体设备和工作流程应用组织的终端、文件访问、代理和数据防泄漏控制。处理数据前，请确认这些控制能否阻止敏感信息进入任务。审计日志和合规导出有助于监控与调查，但它们本身不会阻止数据处理。</p> <p>数据的存储与保留方式取决于信息类别及其保存位置。</p> <table> <thead> <tr> <th><strong>信息类别</strong></th> <th><strong>需要检查的事项</strong></th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td>本地对话记录</td> <td>桌面体验如何存储、删除、备份或共享本地记录。不要假设托管对话的保留设置也适用于每一种本地产物。</td> </tr> <tr> <td>本地文件和生成的输出</td> <td>设备存储、终端策略、用户授权的上传、外部共享，以及另外保存的副本。</td> </tr> <tr> <td>提示词、文件片段和应用上下文</td> <td>提供给模型或服务的内容、适用的工作区条款，以及该工作流程实际的数据流向。</td> </tr> <tr> <td>语音和 Appshot</td> <td>麦克风输入、前台窗口截图、可访问的应用文本、本地会话存储，以及作为任务上下文发送的内容。</td> </tr> <tr> <td>浏览器数据</td> <td>涉及的浏览器配置文件、已有登录状态、浏览历史、下载、网站审批，以及另行保存的任务内容。</td> </tr> <tr> <td>已连接系统记录</td> <td>源系统的权限与保留政策、连接账户身份，以及另行保存到对话或其他位置的信息。</td> </tr> <tr> <td>合规和活动记录</td> <td>工作区、受支持的集成和接收系统的保留政策分别支持记录哪些 Work Local 事件。</td> </tr> </tbody> </table> <p>对于受支持的 Business、Enterprise 和 Edu 工作区，由受涵盖的 OpenAI 服务处理的业务数据会在传输中和静态存储时加密；默认情况下，这些数据不会用于训练或改进 OpenAI 模型。这些保护措施不代表 OpenAI 管理每个设备文件、第三方应用、浏览器配置文件或源系统记录。</p> <p>未经确认适用于特定数据类别之前，不要把托管对话、临时上传或合规日志的保留期限套用到本地记录上。</p> <p><a id=\"audit-and-compliance-visibility\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_11\">审计和合规可见性</h2> <p>启用 Work Cloud 本地电脑访问后，现有 OpenTelemetry（OTel）收集器接收的内容会发生变化。本地执行器仍可导出受支持的执行事件，但云端协调事件不会发送到原有 OpenTelemetry 收集器。</p> <p>受支持的云端记录请使用 Compliance API。更改收集器端点不会让云端协调事件重新出现；Compliance API 记录也不会取代旧版 OpenTelemetry 数据流中的所有事件。请参阅<a href=\"#/article/a-d50d47c21e8fd4ad?anchor=review-opentelemetry-and-audit-coverage\">查看 OpenTelemetry 与审计覆盖范围</a>。</p> <p>可用报告取决于工作区方案、产品体验、事件、已连接应用和部署配置。依赖工作区导出进行事件响应或法规审查前，请先确认 Work Local 的覆盖范围。</p> <p>在受支持的版本中，Browser Use 可以针对功能检查（包括网站访问、上传和下载）发出 OpenTelemetry 事件。这些事件会记录决策及其来源，并在可用时记录策略详情；决策来源也可能是未知。配置导出后，事件会与其他本地运行时遥测一起发送到客户的 OpenTelemetry 端点。</p> <p>OpenTelemetry 导出应与 Compliance API 记录分别配置。这些事件不能证明浏览器或原生应用的每项操作都留下了完整记录。请参阅 <a href=\"#/article/a-b011aa51b3319b85?anchor=observability-and-telemetry\">OpenTelemetry 配置</a>。</p> <p>请确认相关系统是否会记录任务身份、受支持的提示词和回复、已连接应用调用、浏览器审批、应用操作、本地文件活动或终端事件。源系统和设备记录所提供的可见信息，可能与 ChatGPT 工作区记录不同。</p> <p>OpenAI 不会单独保存通过扩展程序执行的 Chrome 操作完整记录。不要假设每次本地文件操作、截图、浏览器操作、审批或外部更新都会出现在 Compliance API 中。</p> <p><a id=\"start-with-one-approved-task\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_12\">从一项已批准的任务开始</h2> <p>请在团队实际使用的受管设备上验证本地 Work。运行一项已批准的任务，例如比较指定的财务工作簿。确认每位用户都有 Work 访问权限，并且只向任务提供所需的文件、应用、浏览器会话或已连接账户。</p> <p>检查已批准的操作能否正常执行、受限操作是否会被拦截，以及可用记录是否满足监控需要。请用户审核结果，并确认对文件或已连接系统所作的任何更改都符合请求。</p>","en":"<h1 id=\"chatgpt-work-local-security\">ChatGPT Work local security</h1> <blockquote> <p>For the complete documentation index, see <a href=\"https://learn.chatgpt.com/llms.txt\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">llms.txt (online)</a>. Markdown versions of documentation pages are available by appending <code>.md</code> to the page URL.</p> </blockquote> <p>ChatGPT Work can use approved files, applications, and browser sessions on a user's computer to complete local tasks. Access depends on workspace permissions, the user's existing account access, operating-system permissions, application approvals, and supported device policies.</p> <p>For an overview of the security controls, see the <a href=\"https://cdn.openai.com/pdf/chatgpt-work-enterprise-security.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">ChatGPT Work enterprise security whitepaper (online)</a>.</p> <p>Local capabilities depend on the supported desktop app, operating system, workspace entitlement, role permissions, device policy, and product rollout.</p> <h2 id=\"security-at-a-glance\">Security at a glance</h2> <ul> <li> <p>Local steps run through the ChatGPT desktop app. In Local computer access with Work Cloud, OpenAI's cloud coordinates the task while the connected computer executes steps that require it.</p> </li> <li> <p>Available local and hosted Work controls depend on workspace configuration and rollout.</p> </li> <li> <p>File access, Computer Use, browsers, and connected apps use different permissions and approvals.</p> </li> <li> <p>A browser or application already signed in to a company system can expose the permissions of that existing account.</p> </li> <li> <p>Supported managed-device policies can restrict local features without replacing workspace access controls.</p> </li> <li> <p>Business, Enterprise, and Edu workspace data processed by covered OpenAI services is encrypted in transit and at rest and is not used to train OpenAI models by default.</p> </li> <li> <p>Local files, task context, browser data, connected-system records, and audit events can follow different storage and retention rules.</p> </li> </ul> <h2 id=\"where-local-tasks-run\">Where local tasks run</h2> <p>Local execution accesses approved resources through the desktop app on the user's computer. With Local computer access with Work Cloud enabled, OpenAI's cloud coordinates the task and calls the connected computer when a step needs local execution. The computer must be online and connected for those steps.</p> <p>Local computer access with Work Cloud applies only to tasks created after you enable sync. Existing tasks, including tasks in projects, keep their original mode: locally only, or in the cloud without access to local files. Start a new task to use this feature.</p> <p>If the computer is unavailable when a new turn starts, an existing eligible task using local computer access with Work Cloud can continue in a cloud container. The cloud container cannot access files or tools on the unavailable computer. It also does not enforce enterprise requirements from local execution. A task cannot switch from local execution to the cloud during a turn.</p> <p>Local files can remain on the device, but relevant file excerpts, prompts, screenshots, browser content, or tool results may be sent to OpenAI services to complete a task. Local execution does not mean offline or device-only model inference.</p> <h2 id=\"files-and-device-access\">Files and device access</h2> <p>A local task can work with information the user provides or makes available, including supported files, application content, browser sessions, and authorized connected systems. Access depends on the user's existing privileges and the controls governing that specific capability.</p> <p>Granting local Work access does not automatically approve every application, grant administrator rights, or bypass the permissions of the account used to reach another system. An approved shared connection can have different privileges from the user's personal account.</p> <p>Direct file reads, file edits, and shell commands follow the task's sandbox and approval settings. Computer Use accesses content through an approved native application under that application's permissions. A restriction on direct file access does not by itself establish the same restriction on files the application can open.</p> <h2 id=\"computer-use-and-application-approvals\">Computer Use and application approvals</h2> <p>Workspace permissions determine who can use Work Local. Administrator policies can further restrict which native applications <a href=\"#/article/a-7be3f970bd43699c\">Computer Use</a> may operate and whether application approvals can be saved for future sessions. Required operating-system permissions and application approvals still apply. When a task uses an application or signed-in account, that account's permissions determine which information and actions are available.</p> <p>Allowing an application through administrator policy does not install the required plugin, grant operating-system permissions, or approve an action that still requires review. For supported controls and configuration examples, see <a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e?anchor=control-browser-and-computer-use\">Managed browser and Computer Use controls</a>.</p> <p>On macOS, Screen Recording allows Computer Use to see application content, and Accessibility allows it to click, type, and navigate. Supported macOS tasks can run in the background. On Windows, Computer Use operates on the active, visible desktop and cannot run in the background while the user continues using that same session.</p> <p>Users can stop a task at any time. Computer Use cannot approve operating-system security prompts, authenticate as an administrator, or automate terminal applications or ChatGPT itself.</p> <h3 id=\"locked-devices\">Locked devices</h3> <p>Supported macOS configurations can optionally allow an approved Computer Use task to continue after the Mac locks. Availability depends on the app version, feature rollout, applicable requirements, and remote-control eligibility.</p> <p>Enabling Locked Use installs a macOS authorization plugin that can temporarily unlock the Mac for an active, trusted Computer Use turn. ChatGPT covers every display during the temporary unlock. If it detects local keyboard or pointer input, it relocks the Mac and pauses automatic unlock until the user unlocks it manually. See <a href=\"#/article/a-7be3f970bd43699c?anchor=locked-use\">How Locked Use works</a>.</p> <p>Administrators can prevent users from enabling Locked Use on managed Macs. This requirement does not turn off Locked Use if it is already enabled. Windows Computer Use requires an active, unlocked desktop. See <a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e?anchor=restrict-locked-computer-use\">Locked Use restrictions</a>.</p> <h2 id=\"browser-sessions-and-existing-sign-ins\">Browser sessions and existing sign-ins</h2> <p>Work Local does not automatically gain access to every browser or company account. Access depends on the browser used, the signed-in account, and the approvals required for that browser experience.</p> <table> <thead> <tr> <th>Browser path</th> <th>Session and security boundary</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td><a href=\"#/article/a-a7de5b39bd26a809\">Desktop in-app browser</a></td> <td>Uses a profile separate from the user's regular browser. Users can sign in within that profile. On supported clients, managed Browser Use policies restrict agent access to sites and available browser capabilities. The built-in browser cannot automate file uploads.</td> </tr> <tr> <td><a href=\"#/article/a-a7459570776840aa\">Chrome extension</a></td> <td>Can interact with existing tabs and signed-in accounts when the extension and website access are approved. On supported clients, the same managed Browser Use policies apply to agent actions through the extension.</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Computer Use operating a browser</td> <td>Operates the browser as an approved native application and can use accounts already signed in. <strong>Browser Use's site and capability policies do not apply to this path.</strong> Native-app policies, operating-system permissions, application approvals, and the signed-in account's permissions still apply.</td> </tr> </tbody> </table> <p>Supported Browser Use policies can restrict site access, uploads, downloads, browser-history access, and full Chrome DevTools Protocol (CDP) access for browser debugging. Administrators can also restrict automatic approval review, saved approvals, and how long site-access approvals last. A user approval cannot override an administrator's denial. See <a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e?anchor=control-browser-and-computer-use\">Managed browser controls</a>.</p> <p>A hosted cloud browser is separate from the user's local browsers and does not automatically inherit their existing sign-ins. Website sign-in through the cloud browser is not available in Enterprise or Edu workspaces.</p> <h2 id=\"apps-plugins-and-connected-accounts\">Apps, plugins, and connected accounts</h2> <p>A connected app can provide access to information or actions in another system. A plugin can use an app as an underlying tool. Making a plugin available does not automatically enable the required app, authorize an account, or permit every action.</p> <p>Plugin and app defaults depend on the plan and whether the workspace is new or existing. New Enterprise and Edu workspaces start with a selected set of apps enabled; those defaults do not change existing workspace settings and do not apply to Healthcare workspaces. In general, new plugins and apps are disabled by default in Enterprise and Edu. Business apps are enabled by default. Administrators can change availability. See the <a href=\"https://help.openai.com/en/articles/11509118\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">current plugin and app defaults (online)</a> and the <a href=\"#/article/a-18ad336d66dd572e\">ChatGPT Work overview</a>.</p> <p>Before a task uses a connected system, confirm that the workspace allows the app and any required plugin, the connection is authorized, and the connected account can access the requested information or action. Read-only settings, allowed actions, and confirmation requirements vary by integration.</p> <p>Plugins that provide browser or Computer Use capabilities require their own availability and installation review. Making a plugin available does not override managed browser or native-app policies, operating-system permissions, or required approvals. Desktop-only plugins and locally provided tools may follow different installation paths. See <a href=\"#/article/a-20bc98306a44b420\">Plugin controls</a>.</p> <h3 id=\"personal-and-shared-connections\">Personal and shared connections</h3> <p>A personal connection uses the connected user's permissions in the source system. A shared or agent-owned connection uses the connected account's permissions, which can be broader than the user's own access.</p> <p>Limit shared accounts to the necessary data and actions, restrict who can use them, and apply supported action or confirmation controls. Records in the connected system remain subject to that system's permissions and retention policies.</p> <h2 id=\"administrator-access-and-managed-device-policies\">Administrator access and managed-device policies</h2> <p>For Local computer access with Work Cloud, review both the workspace's sync permission and the Agent Security requirements for local execution.</p> <p>For local execution, MDM and legacy managed-device requirements rank above Agent Security. The device's system requirements file ranks below Agent Security. Within a given policy, OS-specific environment overrides take priority over all-OS environment overrides, followed by Global. A higher-priority policy wins even if a lower-priority policy is more specific. Some requirements have field-specific merge rules.</p> <p>Use the policy API to manage Global settings. To manage Local or Codex Cloud settings, use the Agent Security UI. Existing Global API workflows remain available after migration. Test your scripts and Terraform integrations, and confirm that policy assignments and ordering are unchanged.</p> <p>For Work with local access and dots, supported Global policy applies through the shared cloud orchestrator when managed policy is enabled. Applicable local <code>requirements.toml</code> requirements govern execution on a connected computer. Work cloud containers and dots cloud computers use their own execution configuration and requirements, rather than the managed environment bundle used by other executor types. Local execution restrictions do not automatically apply to these cloud computers. Review cloud capability permissions and test local and cloud execution separately.</p> <p>Before enabling sync, review these requirements:</p> <ul> <li> <p><strong>Policies delivered only through MDM. MDM delivers policy to devices. Create an Agent Security baseline for supported local execution requirements before enabling sync.</strong></p> </li> <li> <p><strong>Enterprise hooks.</strong> Where enabled for your workspace, Local computer access with Work Cloud supports admin-defined MCP hooks that run on the cloud coordinator (orchestrator) for supported lifecycle and tool events. Command hooks and hooks from local configuration or plugins are not supported with cloud orchestration, even when tools execute locally. When both orchestration and execution are local, existing supported hooks continue to work in local-only Work and Codex threads. Admins can still configure supported managed hooks in Agent Security for those workflows.</p> </li> <li> <p><strong>Auditing.</strong> Before relying on these hooks, test the callback connection, confirm the events it receives, and check how failures affect the task. MCP hooks do not provide a complete Compliance API audit trail.</p> </li> </ul> <p>Review the Work controls available in <strong>Workspace settings</strong> &gt; <strong>Permissions &amp; roles</strong>. Whether local and hosted Work appear as distinct permissions depends on the workspace configuration and rollout. For more guidance, see the <a href=\"#/article/a-b498643ba7f3888a\">Work administrator FAQ</a>.</p> <p>Enable only the execution environments approved for each user or group, and verify effective access after making changes.</p> <p>Workspace permissions determine who can use Work. Administrators can also restrict supported desktop capabilities through enforced requirements defined in <code>requirements.toml</code>. Depending on the deployment, these requirements can be delivered through workspace-managed configuration, a system-level configuration file, or supported macOS mobile device management tools.</p> <p>Enforced requirements cannot be overridden by individual users. Managed defaults, by contrast, establish initial settings that users may be able to change. Neither replaces workspace roles or operating-system permissions.</p> <p>For local clients that use the existing local configuration path, managed requirements load during configuration loading. A background refresh can prepare cloud requirements for a later load, and macOS MDM changes are read at the next client launch. Restart and verify the applicable local client after a local policy change.</p> <p>Turning off Local computer access with Work Cloud interrupts currently running turns. Users can start a new turn in an existing cloud conversation. That turn automatically uses Work Cloud without access to local files.</p> <p>This local loading behavior does not establish when a cloud policy change takes effect in a running task using Local computer access with Work Cloud. Verify the task's effective controls before relying on the update.</p> <table> <thead> <tr> <th><strong>Managed setting</strong></th> <th><strong>Security purpose</strong></th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td><code>features.computer_use = false</code></td> <td>Disable native Computer Use capabilities.</td> </tr> <tr> <td><code>allow_appshots = false</code></td> <td>Prevent supported Appshot capture.</td> </tr> <tr> <td><code>features.in_app_browser = false</code></td> <td>Disable the desktop app's built-in browser.</td> </tr> <tr> <td><code>features.browser_use = false</code></td> <td>Disable supported Browser Use capabilities. External Browser Use has a separate flag.</td> </tr> <tr> <td><code>features.browser_use_external = false</code></td> <td>Disable agent-driven Browser Use through supported browser extensions.</td> </tr> <tr> <td><code>features.apps = false</code> or <code>features.plugins = false</code></td> <td>Restrict supported connected applications or plugins.</td> </tr> <tr> <td><code>computer_use.allow_locked_computer_use = false</code></td> <td>Prevent users from enabling Locked Use on managed Macs. This does not disable existing Locked Use.</td> </tr> </tbody> </table> <p>These are restriction examples, not a list of enabled defaults. Omitting a feature requirement leaves normal client, platform, rollout, and user settings in effect. Browser Use feature flags, the built-in browser pane, and native Computer Use are separate controls. Check each browser path independently.</p> <p>Available settings and delivery methods depend on the client, operating system, workspace, and deployment configuration. Check restrictions on a representative managed device. For supported policy settings, configuration examples, and MDM setup instructions, see <a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e\">Managed configuration</a>.</p> <h2 id=\"local-networking-and-private-resources\">Local networking and private resources</h2> <p>Work Local can reach company information through a browser, a native application, a connected app, or another supported tool. Network controls apply differently to each path, and access to a corporate VPN does not itself authorize a task to use an internal resource.</p> <p>Managed network requirements and Browser Use origin policies are separate checks. Check both on the app versions and operating systems in your deployment before relying on a network restriction to limit Browser Use. Browser policy checks do not mean browser traffic is routed through the command-network proxy, and Browser Use policies do not govern traffic from native applications.</p> <p>Managed network requirements under <code>[experimental_network]</code> are experimental and may change. Windows support is limited; test the exact client and environment before applying them to Windows users. Domain rules alone do not activate the managed proxy; <code>experimental_network.enabled = true</code> is required and does not override a sandbox that keeps networking off.</p> <p>Review the connection, signed-in account, destination, and action required by the workflow. For configuration details and platform limitations, see <a href=\"#/article/a-77481efb12a63f4e?anchor=configure-network-access-requirements\">Network access requirements</a>.</p> <h2 id=\"data-handling-and-retention\">Data handling and retention</h2> <p>For Local computer access with Work Cloud, cloud coordination is part of the workflow even when a step runs on the computer. Review conversation data and task context separately from the local files used by a tool. Do not infer that local execution means device-only storage or that every file in a folder is uploaded.</p> <p>Local computer access with Work Cloud does not provide strict zero data retention. Data residency and inference residency cover only eligible content and supported workloads, regions, and configurations. Enterprise Key Management (EKM) covers supported stored content in eligible workspaces. Work is not supported with UAE inference residency. Confirm the workspace's storage, retention, and audit coverage before rollout. If your organization requires ZDR, do not enable this feature. If <code>enforce_residency</code> is enabled in any cloud policy, <strong>Allow local computer access</strong> is disabled for both Work and dots. This safeguard does not configure workspace residency or, by itself, disable Work Cloud or dots.</p> <p>Apply your organization's endpoint, file-access, proxy, and data-loss-prevention controls to the specific device and workflow. Confirm whether those controls can prevent sensitive information from entering the task before processing. Audit logs and compliance exports help with monitoring and investigation but do not block processing on their own.</p> <p>Storage and retention depend on the information category and where it is saved.</p> <table> <thead> <tr> <th><strong>Information category</strong></th> <th><strong>What to review</strong></th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td>Local conversation records</td> <td>How the desktop experience stores, deletes, backs up, or shares local records. Do not assume hosted-conversation retention settings apply to every local artifact.</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Local files and generated outputs</td> <td>Device storage, endpoint policy, user-authorized uploads, external sharing, and any separately saved copies.</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Prompts, file excerpts, and application context</td> <td>Content supplied to a model or service, applicable workspace terms, and the workflow's actual data flow.</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Voice and Appshots</td> <td>Microphone input, frontmost-window screenshots, accessible application text, local session storage, and any content sent as task context.</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Browser data</td> <td>The browser profile involved, existing sign-ins, browsing history, downloads, website approvals, and any separately stored task content.</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Connected-system records</td> <td>Source-system permissions and retention, connected-account identity, and any information separately saved to the conversation or another destination.</td> </tr> <tr> <td>Compliance and activity records</td> <td>Which Work Local events are available for the workspace, the supported integration, and the receiving system's retention policy.</td> </tr> </tbody> </table> <p>For supported Business, Enterprise, and Edu workspaces, business data processed by covered OpenAI services is encrypted in transit and at rest and is not used to train or improve OpenAI models by default. These protections do not mean OpenAI governs every device file, third-party application, browser profile, or source-system record.</p> <p>Do not apply a hosted-conversation, temporary-upload, or compliance-log retention period to local records without confirming that it applies to the specific data category.</p> <h2 id=\"audit-and-compliance-visibility\">Audit and compliance visibility</h2> <p>Enabling Local computer access with Work Cloud changes what your existing OpenTelemetry (OTel) collector receives. Your local executor can still export supported execution events. Cloud orchestration events do not reach your existing OpenTelemetry collector.</p> <p>Use the Compliance API for supported cloud records. Changing the collector endpoint does not restore cloud orchestration events. Compliance API records do not replace every event in the earlier OpenTelemetry stream. See <a href=\"#/article/a-d50d47c21e8fd4ad?anchor=review-opentelemetry-and-audit-coverage\">Review OpenTelemetry and audit coverage</a>.</p> <p>Available reporting depends on the workspace plan, product experience, event, connected application, and deployed configuration. Verify Work Local coverage before relying on a workspace export for incident response or regulatory review.</p> <p>In supported versions, Browser Use can emit OpenTelemetry events for capability checks, including site access, uploads, and downloads. These events record the decision and decision source, with policy details where available. The decision source can be unknown. When export is configured, they are sent to the customer's OpenTelemetry endpoint alongside other local-runtime telemetry.</p> <p>Configure OpenTelemetry export separately from Compliance API records. These events do not establish a complete record of every browser or native-app action. See <a href=\"#/article/a-b011aa51b3319b85?anchor=observability-and-telemetry\">OpenTelemetry configuration</a>.</p> <p>Determine whether the relevant systems record the task identity, supported prompts and responses, connected-app calls, browser approvals, application actions, local file activity, or endpoint events. Source-system and device records can provide different visibility from ChatGPT workspace records.</p> <p>OpenAI does not store a separate complete record of Chrome actions performed through the extension. Do not assume that every local file operation, screenshot, browser action, approval, or external update appears in the Compliance API.</p> <h2 id=\"start-with-one-approved-task\">Start with one approved task</h2> <p>Verify local Work on the managed devices your teams use. Run an approved task, such as comparing selected finance workbooks. Confirm each user's Work access and provide only the files, applications, browser sessions, or connected accounts the task requires.</p> <p>Check that approved actions work, restricted actions are blocked, and available records meet your monitoring needs. Have the user review the results and confirm that any changes to files or connected systems match the request.</p>"};
